  "insight": {
    "title": "You Are Imagining the Ancient World in the Wrong Color",
    "subtitle": "Why the \"classic\" look of white marble was actually a historical accident.",
    "readingTime": 2,
    "sections": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Every Roman statue you have ever seen was originally painted in loud, garish colors. We imagine a world of white marble, but the ancients actually lived in a landscape of bright, vivid paint."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "Over centuries, the colors weathered away, leaving only the bare stone. When Renaissance artists rediscovered these statues, they mistakenly assumed the white marble was a deliberate choice of a pure and clean style."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "This error defined how we build today. We constructed our most serious government buildings in plain white because we didn't realize the originals actually looked more like a vibrant, colorful carnival."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "text": "We built our world to match a ruin, not a civilization."
      }
    ]
  },
